  "full_text": "So programming demands high precision and high precision is tedious to listen to and a nobleman often doesn't complete every sentence in high precision. It leaves a little bit unsaid. He even fudges things that aren't quite high precision in the interest of the pain of listening to a... peasant get something out of his mouth. So, TempleOS is noble in some respects when it comes down to it. I wanted it really short and precise. The difference in TempleOS and other operating systems is a noble tongue. It makes a difference to the blue tongue. And I was vain about my code itself. That comes from my childhood when I studied the Commodore 64 ROM. Now here's a classic, what do you call it, example of itself. The ROM is 20,000 lines. And a scientist trained in precision immediately says, no, it's not 20,000 lines. It's a 20K ROM. in the interest of being a nobleman, fudges it in a way that pounds through in a more effective way than a peasant who would babble about, oh it's 20k and not quite, anyway.",
